          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Where does guidance differ on non-antibiotic management of uncomplicated UTI?",
          "answer": "Guidance differs on the non-antibiotic management of uncomplicated urinary tract infections (UTI), specifically regarding the role of non-antibiotic first-line management for mild cystitis.\n\nSome European guidance suggests offering symptomatic treatment with delayed or no antibiotics as a shared decision, based on evidence that some episodes resolve without antibiotics. Other guidance, however, maintains immediate antibiotics as the default approach, citing concerns about symptom duration and the risk of the infection progressing to kidney involvement. This disagreement centers on how much antibiotic exposure is acceptable to avoid, rather than on the underlying medical facts (guideline-uti-uncomplicated-treatment).\n\nThere is also a difference in opinion on the encouragement of a \"delayed-prescription\" strategy. Some bodies recommend it as a way to reduce immediate antibiotic prescribing, while others are more cautious, noting that it often still results in an antibiotic prescription and might reinforce the idea that antibiotics are always necessary for these illnesses (guideline-uri-antibiotic-stewardship).\n\nCranberry products and increased fluid intake are described as having limited and mixed evidence for their effectiveness (guideline-uti-uncomplicated-treatment).\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
